                <pb facs="0541" n="521" sameAs="frus:frus1864p3/pg521"/>

                <head ana="#head_untitled-missing-head-in-printed-volume">[Untitled]</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">British Consulate</hi>, <hi
                                rendition="#italic">Kanagawa, Japan</hi>,</placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1863-05-14"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >May</hi> 14, 1863.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>On this day and year above written, before me, the undersigned, her Britannic
                    Majesty’s consul for Kanagawa, Japan, personally came Sergeant Simeon Lawrence,
                    of her Majesty’s 67th regiment, who being by me duly sworn, on his oath deposes
                    and says, that on the morning of the 6th instant he heard cries and shouts of a
                    man, and going out from the station, the British new consular buildings and
                    jail, he saw a crowd of he thinks near a dozen Japanese around a foreigner, who
                    was found to be an American citizen, Mr. S. Robertson; that said Japanese were
                    forcibly taking and carrying him away; that with two men he proceeded and
                    rescued Mr. Robertson; that when he did so, Mr. Robertson’s shirt was torn and
                    hanging down around his legs, and he had no coat, vest, or hat on, and one of
                    the Japanese had holding over him an iron hook, or hatchet-like instrument; that
                    a part of the Japanese ran away, but no arrests were made, as Mr. Robertson knew
                    the men, and said he would make complaint, and have an officer make the
                    necessary arrests through the American consul.</p>
                <p rendition="#right">SIMEON LAWRENCE.</p>
                <p>Subscribed and sworn to before me, the day and year above written.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>CHARLES A. WINCHESTER, <hi rendition="#italic">Her Britannic Majesty’s
                            Consul.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
